% sezione (data)
\section{Lesson 24-09-26}

% stili pagina
\thispagestyle{empty}
\pagestyle{fancy}

% testo
We continue treating \textit{active components}, starting from the \textbf{current-voltage} characteristic of the Diode:
\begin{definition}{Schottky's law}
The current-voltage characteristic of a diode is:
$$
i_D(t) = I_S \left( e^{\frac{v_D(t)}{\eta V_T}} - 1 \right)
$$
\end{definition}

Where:
\begin{itemize}
	\item $I_S$ is the \textit{saturation current}, interpreted as the maximum reverse current we can force through the diode;
	\item $\eta$ is the \textit{ideality factor}, between $1$ and $3$ (usually simplified to $1$);
	\item $V_T$ is the \textit{voltage equivalent of temperature}, around 26 millivolts.
\end{itemize}

Of course, the breakdown effect experienced by the diode for $v_D(t) << 0$ isn't taken into account in Schottky's law.
We usually take approximated models where the diode starts acting like a voltage source if the revers current conduction constraint is satisfied.

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/diode_char.png}
\end{center}

Diodes can be \textit{modeled} in a variety of simplified ways:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Ideal switch}: when voltage drop is negative, current is $0$; when current is positive, the voltage drop is $0$;
	\item \textbf{Ideal switch} with \textit{offset}: when voltage drop is less than $V_\gamma$, the current is $0$; when current is positive, the voltage drop is $V_\gamma$;
	\item \textbf{Ideal switch} with \textit{dynamic resistance}: when voltage drop is less than $V_\gamma$, the current is $0$; whn voltage drop is more than $V_\gamma$, the current follows a resistive law. This is equivalent to applying a small series resistance when the ideal diode is conducting.
\end{itemize}

The way circuits are solved by using approximate models is by first making an \textit{assumption} about the state of a diode (conducting or not), and then checking that assumption.
Since the models have 2 states, for a diode we can assume that if it's not one state, it's the other.
For $n$ diodes we have to make $n$ choices, and thus explore a $2^n$ assumption space.

\textbf{\textsf{BJT transistors}} are active semiconductor components, commonly used as amplifiers or current controlled switches.
They also are a non-linear component, built from 2 PN junctions put head-to-head (which we know on their own made diodes).
Two types of BJT transistors exist: PNP and NPN.
NPNs are more common.

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/bjt.png}
\end{center}

The BJT transistor is \textit{tripole} made up of:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Emitter}: emits charge carriers scaled by the base current;
	\item \textbf{Base}, used to control the device via current;
	\item \textbf{Collector}, collects charge carriers emitted by the emitter.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/bjt_char.png}
\end{center}

BJTs work in 3 main regions:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Forward active} region, where the collector current is $\beta i_B$, so a direct scaling of the base current by a gain factor $\beta$. The voltage drop of the base on the emitter is taken with the usual diode law as $V_\gamma \approx 0.7 V$. A \textit{reverse active} region also exists but is usually undesirable;
	\item \textbf{Cut-off} region, where the collector current is 0 and the BJT acts as an open switch;
	\item \textbf{Saturation} region, where the $v_{CE}$ between collector and emitter is approximately 0 (around $0.1 \sim 0.2 V$). In this region the collector cannot give more current and the BJT acts as a closed switch.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textsf{MOS transistors}} are also active semiconductor components, like BJTs, commonly used as amplifiers or current controlled switches.
They use a different technology to obtain basically the same characteristics: historically they were proposed around the time BJTs started becoming popoular, but weren't implemented until much later.

The main difference is that MOS transistors act as \textit{voltage controlled}, instead of \textit{current controlled} devices.

MOSes consist, briefly, of three superimposed layers:

\begin{itemize}
	\item A base layer of doped semiconductor, which is usually silicon (Si) doped P; 
	\item An intermediate oxide layer, which historically was silicon oxide ($SiO_2$) and today is mainly polysilicate. In general, we assume that the oxide is a perfect insulator (in reality, it never is, but this approximation is useful to us);
	\item An upper metal layer, which historically was aluminum (Al).
\end{itemize}

There are then two additional electrodes, connected to N-doped regions in the P-type substrate. We call these electrodes \textbf{source} and \textbf{drain}. At this point, the bottom electrode $G$ is called \textbf{gate}.

We can see that between the P-type substrate and the two N-type electrodes (source and drain), \textit{diodes} are essentially formed, preventing current from flowing from the source and drain into the substrate.

For the MOSFET to operate, therefore, the N-doped regions need to extend beneath (or at least slightly overlap) the oxide. We call the distance $L$ between the two N-doped regions the \textit{gate length}. 

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/mos.png}
\end{center}

So, we can say that a MOS transistor is another \textit{tripole} made up of:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Source}: emits charge carriers scaled by the base voltage;
	\item \textbf{Gate}, used to control the device via voltage, it is always isolated from drain and source;
	\item \textbf{Drain}, collects charge carriers emitted by the emitter.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/mos_char.png}
\end{center}

MOSes work in 3 main regions:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Cut-off} region: when the $v_{GS}$ voltage between gate and source is lower than a certain $v_{th}$ threshold voltage, the drain current is zero and the MOS behaves as an open circuit;
	\item \textbf{Triode} or \textit{linear} region: when the $v_{GS}$ voltage is higher than $v_{th}$, but $v_{DS}$ is less than $v_{GS} - v_{th}$, that is:
$$
v_{GS} > v_{th}, \quad v_{DS} < v_{GS} - v_{th}
$$
then we are in a linear region where current scales mostly directly with voltage applied to $v_{DS}$. The exact law is:
$$
i_D = \mu C_{ox} \frac{W}{L} \left( (v_{GS} - v_{th}) v_{DS} - \frac{v_{DS}^2}{2} \right)
$$
where $C_{ox}$ is the oxidation coefficient of the oxide (usually a polysilicate), and $W$ and $L$ are respectively the width and the length of the MOS gate channel;
\item \textbf{Saturation} region:  when the $v_{GS}$ voltage is higher than $v_{th}$, but $v_{DS}$ is higher than $v_{GS} - v_{th}$, that is:
$$
v_{GS} > v_{th}, \quad v_{DS} > v_{GS} - v_{th}
$$
then we are in saturation, where the current scales directly with $v_{GS}$ indipendently of $v_{DS}$. The exact law is:
$$
i_D = \frac{1}{2} \mu C_{ox} \frac{W}{L} \left( v_{GS} - v_{th} \right)^2
$$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textsf{Operational amplifiers}} are complex components comprised of DC-coupled differential amplifiers with very high gain values.

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/opamp.png}
\end{center}

What OP amps basically do is take the difference in voltage between two terminals (usually labeled $+$ and $-$) and amply that difference with an extremely high open loop gain ($G_{OL}$).

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/opamp_char.png}
\end{center}

This means the output characteristic is:
$$
v_{out} = G_{OL} (v^+ - v^-) = G_{OL} v_D
$$
Let us note that, even if $G_{OL}$ is in the order of several millions, an OP amp is a powered device which requires power and ground rails, and that cannot return voltages lower than the ground rail or higher than the power rail (a phonmenon known as \textit{saturation}).

They are most commonly used as a \textbf{linear amplifier}:
\begin{itemize}
\item With a negative feedback network;
\item Forced to work in linear region;
\item Lower final gain, larger bandwidth, stability.
\end{itemize}

Or used as a \textbf{comparator}:
\begin{itemize}
\item With a positive feedback network (or no feedback);
\item Forced to work in saturation regions.
\end{itemize}

They are widely used in many embedded systems.

Below, a few common circuits with OP amps are noted.
\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.2]{../figures/opamps.png}
\end{center}

We note that transresistance amplifiers will be useful to us when we need to implement analog converters.

\textbf{\textsf{Generalized analog adders / subtractors}} can be designed via OP amps by connecting inputs we want to add to the non inverting input and inputs we want to subtract to the inverting input.

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.2]{../figures/add_sub_analog.png}
\end{center}

Let's make sure to clearly define what we're after before delving into the implementation details.
We ideally want a network based on a negative feedback OP amp amplifier, with a single $R_f$ resistance as the feedback network.
\begin{itemize}
	\item On the non-inverting input, we'll connect in parallel $n$ voltage generators $v_1'$, $v_2'$, ..., $v_n'$, with series resistances of $R_1'$, $R_2'$, ..., $R_n'$;
	\item On the inverting input, we'll connect in parallel a $m$ voltage generators $v_1''$, $v_2''$, ..., $v_n''$, with series resistances of $R_1''$, $R_2''$, ..., $R_n''$.
\end{itemize}

We want the output of this network to perform the following operation on the voltage sources, taken as inputs:
$$
V_{out} = \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' - \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i'' v_i''
$$

\begin{itemize}
	\item 
Let's recall that the inverting input gain can be taken as:
$$
{V_{out}}_j'' = - \frac{R_f}{R_j''} v_j''
$$
	\item
Whereas the non-inverting input gain can be taken as:
$$
{V_{out}}_j' = \left( 1 + \frac{R_f}{ \parallel_{i = 1}^m R_i'' } \right) V_j'
$$
\end{itemize}

The idea is to choose the resistances $R_i'$ and $R_i''$ such that the expression of $V_{out}$ above holds.

\begin{itemize}
\item
Choosing the resistances on the inverting side is really easy as we can simply say:
$$
R_i'' = \frac{R_f}{A_i''}
$$
and in the inverting input gain equation we get the correct gain; 
\item
For the inverting side, we first compute the voltage on node $V^+$ through Millman's theorem (theorem 1.2, of which we gave a proof in section 1.2.5):
$$
V^+ = \frac{ \sum_{i = 1}^n \frac{v_i'}{R_i'} }{ \sum_{i = 1}^n \frac{1}{R_i'} }
$$

From this equation we see that a good starting point is setting:
$$
R_i' = \frac{R_f}{A_i''}
$$
so that the gains at the numerator are correct:
$$
V^+ = \frac{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' }{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' }
$$

Now we use the non-inverting input gain to compute the output of the non-inverting side:
$$
V_{out}' = V^+ \left( 1 + \frac{R_f}{ \parallel_{i = 1}^m R_i'' } \right) =
\frac{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' }{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' } \left( 1 + \frac{R_f}{ \frac{1}{ \sum_{i = 1}^m \frac{1}{R_i''} } } \right)
$$
$$
=
\frac{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' }{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' } \left( 1 + R_f \sum_{i = 1}^m \frac{1}{R_i''} \right) =
\frac{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' }{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' } \left( 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i'' \right)
$$

At this point we impose the output be equal to the positive side of the original output equation:
$$
\frac{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' }{ \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' } \left( 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i'' \right) =
\sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' v_i' \implies
\frac{1 + \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i''}{\sum_{i = 1}^n A_i'} = 1
$$

\end{itemize}

This last equation we obtained is what we have to satisfy in order to get the correct resistor values.
It's pretty simple to make the result right by just adding a resistance on the inverting or non-inverting path based on the value of the left hand side, which we'll call $\alpha$:
$$
\alpha = \frac{1 + \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i''}{\sum_{i = 1}^n A_i'}, \quad
\begin{cases}
	A_{n + 1}' = 1 - \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' + \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i'' \\
	A_{m + 1}'' = -1  + \sum_{i = 1}^n A_i' - \sum_{i = 1}^m A_i''
\end{cases}
$$

\subsection{Logic gates}

We can move on to digital electronics concept, by introducing \textbf{logic gates}.
A logic gate is an electronic component which performs some sort of operation on digital signals.

A simple example of a logic gate is an \textbf{inverter}.
The simplest inverter is given by a \textit{resistive load} inverter.

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.2]{../figures/resist_load.png}
\end{center}

What happens in the circuit is that if we raise $v_{in}$, and thus $v_{GS}$, above the threshold voltage $v_{th}$, then we get conduction betweeen the drain and source of the MOS transistors, and the circuit acts as an open switch.
If we then sample $v_{out}$, we see the voltage after the fall on $R_{pu}$ (which will be close to zero).


In the other two graphs we see two non idealities of this circuit:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Asymmetric} response: the falling edge is much sharper than rising edge, as the $R_{DS}$ of the NMOS is much smaller than $R_{pu}$;
	\item We have \textbf{static} power consumption in the on state (when output is at logic 0), as current flows from drain to source:
		$$
		P = \frac{v_{DD}^2}{R_{DS} + R_{pu}}
		$$
	\item We also have a certain amount of \textbf{dynamic} power consumption, given by the transients happening at state changes. These rely on equivalent capacitances of wires and MOS transistors, and will be studied in more detail later.
\end{itemize}

We can then see that we need a better design, at least in term of response alignment, and absolutely without static power consumption.

